Exercise on Referral service at the Lemington Centre ends for people with long term health conditions
For the last eleven years the referral programme has supported many individuals, all of whom suffer from long term health conditions such as heart disease and diabetes which benefit from regular physical activity.
Last year Public Health and the Clinical Commissioning Group provided some lifeline gap funding for this service.
HealthWORKS has worked very hard over the past three years to avoid this action but are simply unable to maintain operating at the level of loss incurred by running this service at Lemington without any subsidy.
Sarah Cowling , CEO Healthworks Newcastle says “I would like to reassure our existing clients that support will continue up until 22 December 2016, when the current programme will cease altogether; and that from January 2017 all graduates of the programme will be offered the opportunity to continue to exercise during supervised sessions at the Lemington Centre Community Gym each day: Monday to Friday between 10am -12 noon.
Due to the long term support of funding from Newcastle Public Health, the charity is able to continue to run their successful exercise on referral service from the Health Resource Centre in Benwell, welcoming referrals for anyone who is registered with a GP in the inner west of the city.
‘Great course, great staff, great venue!
Quit smoking to reduce chances of the ‘suffocating’ lung disease.”
The “Every Breath” campaign from Fresh; Smoke Free North East, is raising awareness that smoking causes chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and is being supported by the British Lung Foundation.
Sufferers can end up feeling like they are suffocating and one major study suggests at least 1 in 4 smokers will develop clinically significant COPD.
The area also has a high rate of people being admitted to hospital because of COPD.
There are varying levels of severity but once the damage is done, it is irreversible.
Ailsa Rutter, Director of Fresh, said: “COPD is a disease that too many smokers are not aware of until they are diagnosed, but research suggests that more than a quarter of smokers will develop it.
Not being able to breathe properly is something nobody wants to happen to them.
There is plenty of free, friendly, advice and support available to get your quit attempt underway if you live or work in Newcastle.
