There have been many types of military helmets over the years, as seen in this video by Crazy Hydraulic Press.
They first did a controlled experiment using an average worker’s hard hat. Since this particular helmet aims to prevent head-related accidents in construction areas, it only makes sense that it should be the strongest in this situation.
With the control out of the way, it was time to put the military helmets to the test:
Unlike the hard hat, which neatly folded into itself, the steel helmet crumbled into pieces. These jagged parts were sharp enough to scratch the hydraulic press, so imagine the damage it could do to an exposed human head.
Funnily enough, the final experiment showed that a riot helmet could withstand just as much pressure as the Kevlar helmet, despite being made of hard plastic. 1,815kg of pressure is nothing to laugh at, especially if it’s your head under that helmet.
Still, it’s always fun to see stuff get crushed by a hydraulic press. If you want to see more industrial-grade destruction, check out the Crazy Hydraulic Press YouTube channel.
